SCTP association creation: keep the verdict and say WHY it failed.

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the ninth patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes,
0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer, 0004-dtls-diagnostics,
0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing, 0006-dtls-fingerprint-and-verify,
0007-ice-timeout-and-verify-none and 0008-dtls-bio-remb-and-candidate-dedup).

Why
---

The SCTP association is created right after the DTLS handshake succeeds
(src/peer_connection.c: the PEER_CONNECTION_CONNECTED branch).  Until now
the result of that creation was COMPLETELY invisible:

  * sctp_create_association() returned -1 and nothing else -- the errno was
    set to EINVAL and then swallowed by the caller;

  * the SCTP diagnostic channel (sctp_diag_log(), src/sctp.c) never said
    anything, because the FUNCTION THAT SWITCHES IT ON
    (nx_webrtc_backend_init() -> peer_init() -> usrsctp_init_nothreads())
    was never called at all -- see the outer patch 0041.  The engine's
    callback also had to strip the "SCTP " prefix (src/sctp.c uses 5
    characters, peer_connection.c uses "LIBPEER " / 8 characters) and to
    allow the SCTP categories through its filter -- both are in the outer
    patch too;

  * peer_connection_loop() dropped the verdict with the stack frame.

The result was a device log that said

    [peer] INFO  peer_connection.c 1093  SCTP create socket
    [peer] ERROR peer_connection.c 1105  SCTP create socket failed

and NOTHING about why.  The report could not say either.

What
----

1. src/sctp.h
   - a new SctpCreateFailReason enum (the stable reason codes) and a
     `create_fail_reason` field in `struct Sctp`;
   - the declaration of sctp_create_fail_reason_name().

2. src/sctp.c
   - every failure path of sctp_create_association() now records WHY it
     failed: runtime (the usrsctp runtime was never initialised), the
     allocation, the socket, the bind/connect setup, or a null context;
   - sctp_create_fail_reason_name() maps the code to a STABLE string
     ("runtime_not_initialized", "socket_present", "allocation_failed",
     "socket_failed", "setup_failed", "invalid_context", "none",
     "unknown") -- the engine puts that string straight into its getStats
     blob, so it must not change;
   - the "create_failed ..." diagnostic lines carry the reason too.

3. src/peer_connection.h / src/peer_connection.c
   - PeerConnection remembers the verdict (attempts, return code, reason)
     instead of dropping it with the stack frame;
   - a new accessor peer_connection_get_sctp_create_info() exposes it;
   - a second accessor peer_connection_get_sctp_out_queued() exposes the
     depth of the SCTP output ring buffer (0041, cloud review): the queue
     is what sctp_outgoing_data_cb() fills and what sctp_drain() empties,
     so "the queue never drains" says "the INIT never left the device" a
     whole order of magnitude faster than waiting for COMM_UP.

4. src/sctp.c / src/sctp.h
   - sctp_get_output_queue_count(): the ring buffer depth (0 when there is
     no association yet).  The ring is SCTP_OUTPUT_PACKETS deep and
     sctp_outgoing_data_cb() answers EWOULDBLOCK when it is full.

★ Why an ACCESSOR and not three more fields in PeerConnectionDiagnostics:
the engine compiles against extern/libpeer/build/dist/include/peer_connection.h
-- the INSTALLED COPY of the header, not the source one -- and that copy is
only refreshed when libpeer is rebuilt (cmake --install).  A struct that
grows would make the engine and the archive disagree about the layout
without anybody noticing.  A new FUNCTION cannot do that: a stale archive
fails to LINK ("undefined reference to peer_connection_get_sctp_create_info"),
which is loud and immediate.

★ The reason codes are also the ones the DTLS layer trips over: with
srtp_init() never called, libsrtp's crypto kernel stays in
srtp_crypto_kernel_state_insecure, so srtp_crypto_kernel_alloc_cipher()
refuses with srtp_err_status_init_fail and
dtls_srtp_key_derivation() returns -1 (src/dtls_srtp.c:512) -- which is
why dtlsState stayed INIT.  Same root cause, two symptoms.

★ SCTP has no second port: the packets ride inside the DTLS channel that
was just established (out: sctp_drain() -> dtls_srtp_write(); in:
sctp_incoming_data() -> usrsctp_conninput()).  local_port/remote_port =
5000 are the RFC 8841 SCTP-layer ports and are correct -- this patch does
NOT touch them.

Note for the Switch build
-------------------------

extern/libpeer/src/CMakeLists.txt compiles the peer target with
CONFIG_USE_USRSCTP=1 (it is the default in src/config.h too), so the
usrsctp code paths -- and with them sctp_create_association() -- are the
ones that are built.

